From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:22:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(models): export complete asset pairs and empty item
 fallbacks

Java rejects a select model with no cases. Emit minecraft:empty when no
geometry was converted and preserve selectors for actual alternatives.
Only export complete geometry/texture pairs and bind their particle sprite
so Java does not bake dangling texture references.

Server declarations can include unused variants whose images are omitted.
Retain their missing-asset reasons as converter metadata and report an
actively selected custom actor model. An omitted inactive variant does
not prevent joining. Native rendering performs its own active lookup.

Saved Geyser and CubeCraft pack audits contain no dangling sprite or
particle bindings. Semantic tests cover empty and populated item exports,
resolved and unresolved model pairs, and inactive versus active failures.

Keep attachable variant keys relative to their item identifier. The item
content already namespaces model paths by identifier; repeating it in
the variant breaks both the default custom-model selector and readiness
lookup used for equipped items. A recorded Bedrock 1.26.51.1 Hive player
wears angel wings in the chest slot, exposing this mismatch. Semantic
conversion tests resolve both default and folded equipment selectors to
real geometry and matching converter readiness.

Apply resolved attachable models after vanilla and custom item mapping.
A server can override existing armor with an attachable without defining a
new item. Preserve the Java item's type, gameplay components, and native
identity while assigning the converted model selector only after pack
acceptance and successful default geometry/texture conversion. Targeted
tests cover both item families, rejected and missing assets, and preserved
gameplay data. Direct and ViaProxy equipment playback verify the full path.

Retain item-to-attachable binding expressions separately from geometry
conversion. Explicit bindings select a wearer-specific definition before
legacy item-identifier lookup. The pinned 1.26.51.1 definitions distinguish
iron_chestplate from iron_chestplate.player using query.owner_identifier.
The complete actor archive retains these associations for the frontend.
Owner queries remain the renderer's responsibility; additional competing
bindings and default native assets still need broader comparisons.

Reuse compiled geometry across resolved entity texture variants and
attachable aliases within each conversion. Each task owns its model root,
texture bindings and display transforms; shared geometry stays read-only.
Concurrent maps coalesce compilation without retaining models across
connections or introducing a process-wide asset cache.

The matching CubeCraft fixture preserves all 15,823 output entries after
normalizing existing object-hash names, JSON order and equivalent particle
aliases. Warm entity-generation CPU falls from 856 to 664 ms and allocated
bytes from 2201 to 1136 MiB. Overall rewrite time is unchanged in this
fixture, so this reduces CPU and memory pressure without claiming faster
joins. Tests cover concurrent bindings, model groups and coordinates,
scale metadata, repeated conversion and unchanged source geometry.

Export each compiled geometry once as a content-addressed Java parent.
Children retain independent texture bindings and attachable display
transforms. Hash serialized parent bytes instead of a sanitized Bedrock
identifier so distinct geometry cannot overwrite another parent. Keep
texture bindings out of parents so Java resolves each child independently.
The output uses standard model inheritance and works with ordinary Java
clients through ViaProxy without additional client integration.

The five-pack CubeCraft profile retains every original resource and model
selection. Resolving parent chains reproduces all 12,789 changed models;
786 shared parents reduce model JSON from 87.5 to 15.9 MB and the complete
archive from 91.8 to 78.2 MB. In eight conversions with two warm-ups,
entity task CPU falls from 712 to 167 ms and allocations from 1136 to
350 MiB. Rewrite plus ZIP falls from 1739 to 1632 ms. These figures exclude
acquisition, network downloads and client reloads. Parent export tests
cover geometry reuse, distinct parents, source immutability, concurrent
texture bindings, retained model selection, scale and display transforms.

The actual Java 26.3 model parser and inheritance resolver preserve all
changed children, including geometry, texture materials, display
transforms, lighting and ambient occlusion. In a separate nine-iteration
model parsing benchmark with two warm-ups, median parsing falls from
1274 to 254 ms and allocation from 4949 to 786 MiB. This isolates parsing
rather than claiming complete reload or joining improvements.

Use native inventory atlas icons in GUI, ground, fixed and shelf contexts while retaining held attachable geometry. Target item schemas and model tests cover the native spear icon and complete display-context routing.
